  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Johns Hopkins University Applied Physics Laboratory has $1.5B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. Johns Hopkins University Applied Physics Laboratory recorded 694 awards with an average award size of $2.1M, and obligations fell 37.6% year over year. Johns Hopkins University Applied Physics Laboratory draws most of its funding from a small set of agencies: the Department of Defense obligated $972M and the National Aeronautics and Space Administration obligated $470M during the period. Johns Hopkins University Applied Physics Laboratory concentrates activity in Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Nanotechnology and Biotechnology) with $1.3B in obligations, followed by Engineering Services with $77.5M and All Other Professional, Scientific, and Technical Services with $56.2M. Johns Hopkins University Applied Physics Laboratory obligations by fiscal year show a peak of $2.4B in FY25 and a return to $1.5B in FY26. Johns Hopkins University Applied Physics Laboratory metrics here are partial, covering the fiscal year to date, and the note clarifies that tables listing largest awards and expiring contracts show total contract values rather than obligations.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Who are the biggest federal buyers of Johns Hopkins University Applied Physics Laboratory?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the largest buyer, obligating $972M in FY2026 to date. The National Aeronautics and Space Administration is the second largest buyer, obligating $470M. The Department of Homeland Security, Department of Health and Human Services, and Department of Transportation follow with $21.8M, $8M, and $1.7M respectively."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What markets and contract types drive Johns Hopkins University Applied Physics Laboratory revenue?",
        "body": "Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Nanotechnology and Biotechnology) is the dominant market with $1.3B in obligations. Engineering Services and All Other Professional, Scientific, and Technical Services are secondary, with $77.5M and $56.2M respectively. Other notable markets include Other Computer Related Services at $35.7M and Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing at $7.5M."
      }
    ]
